<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acre War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Acrean Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> War was a conflict over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>-rich region of Acre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, then claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> but settled mainly by Brazilian rubber tappers. Brazilian settlers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/placido-de-castro">José Plácido de Castro</a> rose against Bolivian rule and defeated its forces in 1902–1903. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-petropolis">Treaty of Petrópolis</a> of 1903 Bolivia ceded Acre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in return for money and a railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acre-war.jpg" alt="Acre War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathan.rpk · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotos_da_Guerra_do_Acre.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
